  "summary": "Patchworked and seamed squares of Korean cloth — flat-felled seams glowing like stained glass.",
  "description": "Bojagi are Korean wrapping cloths — square, knotted over gifts, food boxes and wedding linens — where leftover silk and ramie scraps are pieced into asymmetric patchwork (jogakbo) whose seams are folded flat and whip-stitched so the cloth reads identically from both sides, light passing through like leaded glass. Royal gift bojagi worked in gold-thread embroidery; commoner bojagi practiced frugality-as-beauty. Contemporary fiber artists worldwide adopt the open-seam technique, and museums collect Joseon bojagi as quiet masterpieces of chance geometry.",
